.SH NAME
ktm-guide-overloading \- Overloading (chapter 18 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Several functions can share one name when their parameter types differ. A call picks the one its arguments fit best, at compile time. This chapter covers declaring overloads, how a call chooses, integer literals and casts, enums, defaults and ties, taking the address of an overload, overloaded methods, and the errors.
.SH Declaring overloads
.PP
Write each version as an ordinary function with the same name and different parameters, in type or in number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void show(i64 n) {
    printf("number %d\en", n);
}

void show(f64 x) {
    printf("float %.2f\en", x);
}

void show(u8 *s) {
    printf("text %s\en", s);
}

void show(i64 a, i64 b) {
    printf("pair %d, %d\en", a, b);
}

i32 main() {
    show(42);
    show(2.5);
    show("hello");
    show(1, 2);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
number 42
float 2.50
text hello
pair 1, 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Overloads usually do the same job for different inputs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 area(i64 side) {
    return side * side;
}

i64 area(i64 width, i64 height) {
    return width * height;
}

f64 area(f64 radius) {
    return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d %.2f\en", area(4), area(3, 5), area(1.0));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
16 15 3.14
.EE
.RE
.SH How a call picks
.PP
Every overload the arguments can be converted to is a candidate. Each argument fits its parameter in one of three ways, from best to worst:
.IP 1. 4
exactly: the same type, or an array or string literal for a slice of its element type;
.IP 2. 4
in the same family: another integer width or signedness, a \fBString\fR for a \fBu8[]\fR, any pointer for a \fBvoid *\fR;
.IP 3. 4
as an integer for a float.
.PP
The overload that fits every argument at least as well as any other, and at least one better, wins. A conversion that would only produce a warning (a pointer given for an integer) doesn't make a candidate.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void take(i64 n) {
    printf("i64\en");
}

void take(f64 x) {
    printf("f64\en");
}

i32 main() {
    i32 small = 3;
    f32 single = 1.5;
    take(small);
    take(single);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i64
f64
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBsmall\fR is an \fBi32\fR: it fits \fBi64\fR in the same family, but \fBf64\fR only as an integer for a float, so \fBtake(i64)\fR wins. \fBsingle\fR fits \fBf64\fR in its family.
.PP
A slice parameter takes arrays and string literals exactly, and a \fBString\fR in its family:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

void describe(u8[] s) {
    printf("%d bytes\en", s.len);
}

void describe(i64 n) {
    printf("the number %d\en", n);
}

i32 main() {
    u8 word[5] = {'h', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'};
    scoped String owned = str.from("owned text");
    describe("literal");
    describe(word);
    describe(owned);
    describe(9);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7 bytes
5 bytes
10 bytes
the number 9
.EE
.RE
.SH Integer literals are i64
.PP
An integer literal is an \fBi64\fR, so it fits an \fBi64\fR parameter exactly and picks it over a narrower one. Cast to choose the other:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void f(i32 x) {
    printf("i32\en");
}

void f(i64 x) {
    printf("i64\en");
}

i32 main() {
    f(5);
    f((i32)5);
    i32 small = 5;
    f(small);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i64
i32
i32
.EE
.RE
.PP
With only narrower overloads, a literal fits each of them equally well, and the call is ambiguous until an argument is cast:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void f(i32 x) {
}

void f(i16 x) {
}

i32 main() {
    f(5);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
the call `f(i64)` is ambiguous: `f(i32)`, `f(i16)` fit as well; cast an argument to pick one
.EE
.RE
.SH Enums are their own type
.PP
An enum member fits a parameter of its enum exactly, so an enum overload and an integer one can live together:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green };

void paint(Color c) {
    printf("color %d\en", c);
}

void paint(i64 n) {
    printf("number %d\en", n);
}

i32 main() {
    paint(Color.green);
    paint(7);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
color 1
number 7
.EE
.RE
.SH Defaults and ties
.PP
When two overloads fit equally well, the one that needs no default arguments and no \fB\&...\fR extras wins:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void send(u8 *msg) {
    printf("plain: %s\en", msg);
}

void send(u8 *msg, i64 retry = 3) {
    printf("retry %d: %s\en", retry, msg);
}

i32 main() {
    send("a");
    send("b", 5);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
plain: a
retry 5: b
.EE
.RE
.SH When nothing fits
.PP
A call that no overload takes is an error that lists them:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void h(i64 x) {
}

void h(u8[] s) {
}

i32 main() {
    h(1.5, 2);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
no overload of `h` takes (f64, i64); there are `h(i64)`, `h(u8[])`
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two functions with the same parameters but different return types aren't overloads, they are a conflict:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
i64 g(i64 x) {
    return x;
}

f64 g(i64 x) {
    return 1.0;
}

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
conflicting types for `g`
.EE
.RE
.SH Overloaded methods
.PP
Methods overload the same way, and so do a module's functions:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

struct Counter { i64 n; };

void Counter.add(i64 by) {
    self.n += by;
}

void Counter.add(u8[] word) {
    self.n += word.len;
}

i32 main() {
    Counter c = {0};
    c.add(3);
    c.add("four");
    scoped String s = str.from("owned");
    c.add(s);
    printf("%d\en", c.n);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
12
.EE
.RE
.PP
A derived struct's method hides every overload of that name in its base (see Methods (see ktm-guide-methods(7))).
.SH Taking the address of an overload
.PP
\fB&name\fR picks the overload whose type the destination asks for: a typed variable, a parameter, a field (see Function pointers (see ktm-guide-function-pointers(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 size(i64 n) {
    return n;
}

i64 size(u8[] s) {
    return s.len;
}

i64 apply(fn(u8[]) i64 f, u8[] s) {
    return f(s);
}

i32 main() {
    fn(i64) i64 byNumber = &size;
    printf("%d %d\en", byNumber(7), apply(&size, "four"));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7 4
.EE
.RE
.PP
With nothing to pick by, it is an error:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
i64 size(i64 n) {
    return n;
}

i64 size(u8[] s) {
    return s.len;
}

i32 main() {
    auto p = &size;
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
`&size` needs the function type to pick one
.EE
.RE
.SH Overloads and generics
.PP
A generic function and plain overloads can share a name; a plain overload that fits exactly is preferred to an instance:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void print<T>(T x) {
    printf("something of %d bytes\en", sizeof(T));
}

void print(u8 *s) {
    printf("text: %s\en", s);
}

i32 main() {
    print("hi");
    print(3.5);
    print(7);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
text: hi
something of 8 bytes
something of 8 bytes
.EE
.RE
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
Functions and methods of one name are overloads when their parameter types differ; return types alone don't make an overload.
.IP \(bu 2
A call takes the overload every argument fits at least as well as with any other: exactly, then the same family, then integer for float. Warning-only conversions don't count.
.IP \(bu 2
On a tie, the overload without defaults or \fB\&...\fR extras wins; otherwise the call is ambiguous and must cast.
.IP \(bu 2
Integer literals are \fBi64\fR; enum members fit their enum exactly.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB&name\fR of an overloaded function needs a destination type.
.IP \(bu 2
An \fBextern "c"\fR function has one C symbol and can't be overloaded; \fB\&...args\fR can't be spread into an overloaded function.
.IP \(bu 2
Overloads with \fB@caller\fR parameters must all start with the same ones.
.IP \(bu 2
Messages show an overload with its parameter types: \fBshow(u8[])\fR\&.
.SH SEE ALSO
